@dnhatn Accessing system indices with the `enrich` origin is fine. What I am suggesting is to add an additional permission check before switching to the `enrich` origin for system indices. This additional permission check could be something like checking whether the user has permission to access the enrich policy. Here is a sample pseudo code for the idea (please note it's meant to facilitate the discussion, not an implementation porposal).

```java
// This is the outer request that is used to check permission for enrich policy
class EnrichRequest extends ActionRequest {
    private final String enrichPolicyName;
}

class EnrichLookupService {
    void lookupAsync(EnrichRequest enrichRequest, ActionListener listener) {
        // Execute in the end-user context
        transportService.sendChildRequest(.., enrichRequest, ...);
    }

    // This is the transport handler method for EnrichRequest
    void handleEnrich(enrichRequest, listener) {
        // When the code reaches here, we can be sure that the enrich policy is accessible to the end-user.
        // We can now stash to use the enrich origin and execute lookupRequest for system indices access
        ...
    }
}
```

The challenge on the security side is that, other than indices, we don't have a great system to authorize requests for sepcific resources. Cluster actions are authorized based on just the action name and not the specific resource it tries to access. That's part of the reason that we don't have fine-grained authorization for things like pipelines. That said, I think it is still useful even if we could only check whether the end-user has a simple cluster wide access to enrich polices.
